<SiteLayout
  title="XYZ Mathematics Club"
  description="A community for exploring mathematics together."
>
  <div
    slot="left-aside"
    class="flex h-full flex-col gap-phi-4 text-meta leading-copy [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a:visited]:text-link-visited [&_h2]:mb-phi-1 [&_h2]:font-bold"
  >
    <section>
      <h2>On this page</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-phi-3">
        <li><a href="#origin">The origin</a></li>
        <li><a href="#first-evening">The first evening</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sessions">What we studied</a></li>
        <li><a href="#community">A community forms</a></li>
        <li><a href="#legacy">The butterfly effect</a></li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-rule pt-phi-2">
      <h2>At a glance</h2>
      <dl class="grid grid-cols-[auto_1fr] gap-x-phi-2 gap-y-phi-0">
        <dt class="font-bold">Founded</dt>
        <dd>Oakhill College</dd>
        <dt class="font-bold">Format</dt>
        <dd>After-hours seminars</dd>
        <dt class="font-bold">Focus</dt>
        <dd>Mathematics beyond the curriculum</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <footer class="mt-auto border-t border-rule pt-phi-2">
      <p>XYZ Mathematics Club archive</p>
      <p>Student-founded. Student-led.</p>
    </footer>
  </div>

  <article class="max-w-reading leading-copy [&_h1]:mb-phi-2 [&_h1]:text-title [&_h1]:font-bold [&_h2]:mb-phi-1 [&_h2]:mt-phi-4 [&_h2]:text-heading [&_h2]:font-bold [&_hr]:my-phi-4 [&_hr]:border-rule [&_p]:my-phi-2">
    <header class="border-b border-rule pb-phi-3">
      <p class="font-mono text-meta">XYZ / CLUB ARCHIVE / HOME</p>
      <h1>XYZ Mathematics Club</h1>
      <p class="text-lede">
        A student-founded forum for studying the mathematics left outside the
        school curriculum—and for making academic curiosity part of the culture.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="origin" aria-labelledby="origin-heading">
      <h2 id="origin-heading">1. The origin</h2>
      <p>
        Oakhill College had a long-standing emphasis on sport. The rugby jocks
        were the cool kids. Mathematics had classrooms and timetables, but it did
        not yet have a community of its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        One day, two best friends asked their HSC Extension 2 Mathematics teacher
        whether they could keep studying after hours in her classroom. Her answer
        was simple: “Yes—so long as it’s maths related.” That was the lightbulb
        moment.
      </p>
      <p>
        The next day, they chose a name, sketched a logo, printed a sign-up sheet,
        and went door-knocking through the top mathematics classes in each year
        group. In thirty minutes, the inaugural XYZ Mathematics Club was at full
        capacity.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="first-evening" aria-labelledby="first-evening-heading">
      <h2 id="first-evening-heading">2. The first evening</h2>
      <p>
        Every registered student arrived. Teachers came too. The premise was not
        exam preparation or accelerated coursework; it was to fill the holes in
        the Australian mathematics curriculum.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="my-phi-3 border-l-4 border-ink pl-phi-3 text-lede">
        <p>
          Few things felt better than watching mathematical intuition suddenly
          click for someone else.
        </p>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        The first session derived differential and integral calculus from first
        principles. As the argument unfolded, there were visible “aha” moments
        around the room—the reason the club existed became immediately clear.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="sessions" aria-labelledby="sessions-heading">
      <h2 id="sessions-heading">3. What we studied</h2>
      <p>
        Sessions moved beyond the syllabus while remaining accessible to curious
        students. Early topics included:
      </p>
      <ul class="my-phi-2 list-disc pl-phi-3 marker:text-ink">
        <li>deriving differential and integral calculus from first principles;</li>
        <li>linear algebra through matrices;</li>
        <li>the geometry and structure behind complex numbers; and</li>
        <li>general frameworks for solving difficult geometry problems.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The subject mattered, but so did the method: derive rather than memorise,
        exchange solutions rather than guard them, and make room for questions
        that did not fit inside a lesson plan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="community" aria-labelledby="community-heading">
      <h2 id="community-heading">4. A community forms</h2>
      <p>
        Students began volunteering to teach. They compared solutions, proposed
        future topics, and learned from one another. What started as permission to
        use a classroom after hours became a durable student community.
      </p>
      <p>
        XYZ was not built by experts with an institutional plan. It was built by
        two maths nerds, an open classroom, and enough shared curiosity to fill it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="legacy" aria-labelledby="legacy-heading">
      <h2 id="legacy-heading">5. The butterfly effect</h2>
      <p>
        It was not long before coding and hardware clubs emerged. The nerds were
        becoming the cool kids. Five years later, the school principal reflected
        that academics had come to occupy a much larger place in Oakhill’s culture
        alongside sport.
      </p>
      <p>
        The club’s legacy is a modest but powerful proposition: give students a
        room, a difficult idea, and permission to take learning seriously, and a
        school’s culture can begin to change.
      </p>
    </section>

    <hr />

    <p class="font-mono text-meta">END OF DOCUMENT / XYZ-001</p>
  </article>

